1) Opening the desktop
----------------------

Type the address you were given in your web browser, something like

  https://your-server/ro/

Your browser asks for a user name and password: that is the server
asking who you are, not RISC OS. Once you are in, the desktop starts
as it does anywhere else. Anyone with that password can use the whole
desktop, including the Server drive and the browser (see 4), so keep
it to yourself.

The page must be opened from the server's address: a copy of the
desktop opened some other way (say from your own computer) is not
connected to the server.


2) The Server drive
-------------------

On the icon bar, on the left, there is a disc called Server. It
appears by itself on every computer you open the desktop on, with
nothing to set up. It is HostFS::Server (see the HostFS guide), so
you use it like any disc: open it, save into it, copy files to it,
run programs from it, *Cat HostFS::Server.$

Server is a folder on the server itself. Files you put there stay
there: close the browser, switch computers, come back next week, and
they are still there. The server's owner keeps a nightly backup of it
(see 7).

To move a file between your computers, save it on Server on one and
open it from Server on the other.


3) What is on each computer, and what is shared
-----------------------------------------------

Shared, the same on every computer you use:

  The Server drive             its files
  !Browse's browser            its logins, cookies and settings
                               (see 4)

Kept by each computer's own web browser, so different on each:

  The hard disc (HardDisc4)    including $.Docs, $.Apps, Choices and
                               anything you saved there
  The RAM disc
  Settings made in !Configure  screen mode, memory, sound and so on
  Folders mounted with !HostFS from that computer

So a file saved on the hard disc at home is not on the hard disc at
work. Put what you want everywhere on Server. Clearing a web
browser's site data on one computer clears that computer's hard disc
too, but never Server.

If two computers change the same file on Server, the last one to save
wins; the other's changes are lost. Close a file on one computer
before editing it on the other.


4) !Browse on a server
----------------------

!Browse shows today's web, drawn by a full browser (Chrome) running
on the server (see the Browse guide). It is out of sight there, and
it is one browser, with one set of logins, cookies and history,
shared by every computer you open the desktop on and by anyone who
has the password. If you log in to a site in !Browse at home, you are
still logged in at work. Log out of sites you do not want shared.

Because the browser is on the server, the pages are fetched from
there, not from your computer. It can reach web sites on the internet.
The server's firewall keeps it away from private addresses, such as
192.168.x.x, and from other machines on the server's own network:
!Browse will say the page cannot be reached. Its own "localhost" is
the server's program itself, which shows at most a bare desktop with
no Server drive, so a web page cannot read or change the files on
the Server drive.

When nobody has the desktop open, the server stops the browser after
a few minutes to save memory. It starts again when you next open
!Browse, which takes a moment longer then.

The text of the Browse guide about the engine only answering the
computer it runs on describes the usual setup. Here a trusted server
set up by its owner answers you instead.


5) Sound
--------

Sound from !Browse's pages may be silent on a server. The desktop's
own sound (!Maestro, games and so on) is made on your computer and is
not affected.


6) Limits and things to know
----------------------------

  * Everyone who knows the password shares Server and !Browse; there
    are no separate users.
  * Downloads in !Browse arrive through the server, so large ones
    take as long as the connection allows.
  * Sites that do not like being used from a server's address, or
    that block automated browsers, may refuse to work.
  * When the server is restarted (for an update, say), !Browse loses
    its connection. Reload the web page: the desktop and !Browse
    reconnect. Unsaved work in !Browse's pages is lost; work in
    other applications is kept by your browser as usual.


7) If you look after the server
-------------------------------

Setting up the server is a job for its owner and is not done from
the desktop. In brief: the desktop runs on the server in a locked-
down container, with a front door (the web server) that asks for a
password and passes requests on; !Browse's browser can reach the
internet but not the owner's own network; Server is a folder on the
server's disc, backed up every night, with the last week's and the
last month's copies kept; the password can be changed at any time.

The full instructions are in the source tree, in deploy/README.md,
and the options the desktop's server takes for this (--public-url,
--browser-idle and the others) are listed in the README.


8) Troubleshooting
------------------

The desktop does not load, or says the server cannot be reached:
check the address, and that you are online. If you were asked for a
password and it is refused, ask the owner.

The Server drive is missing: reload the page. If it is still not
there, the desktop is not connected to the server (see 1); use the
address you were given.

!Browse says the engine is not available from this address: you
opened the desktop some way other than the server's address, or the
server was not set up for !Browse. !Browse then shows pages in a
frame, as described in the Browse guide.

!Browse says the browser engine stopped or the connection was lost:
the server was restarted. Reload the page.
